1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,176
This video runs the full pipeline from start to end. Regex to N F A, N F A to D F A, and D F A to minimal D F A.

2
00:00:10,626 --> 00:00:21,079
The first example is the decimal number regex from the course notes. The second example is the W language with three values, from a past midterm.

3
00:00:21,529 --> 00:00:29,167
A lexer is built from a regex in three steps. Each step has its own algorithm, and each step has a check.

4
00:00:29,617 --> 00:00:38,363
Step 1. The four templates turn the regex into an N F A. Check that each part of the N F A matches one template.

5
00:00:38,813 --> 00:00:49,672
Step 2. The subset construction turns the N F A into a D F A. Check that every state has one arrow per letter. The empty set is the error state.

6
00:00:50,122 --> 00:01:03,114
Step 3. Minimization merges the states that no string can tell apart. The D F A must be total and trim. At the end, test some strings on all the machines and on the regex.

7
00:01:03,564 --> 00:01:16,044
Here is the first example, from the course notes. The letter d is any digit, and the dot is a decimal point. The regex is d star, then dot d or d dot, then d star.

8
00:01:16,494 --> 00:01:22,297
First, read the language. d dot d is a decimal number. The regex matches it.

9
00:01:22,747 --> 00:01:26,928
dot d also matches. The digit after the dot is enough.

10
00:01:27,378 --> 00:01:31,623
d dot also matches. The digit before the dot is enough.

11
00:01:32,073 --> 00:01:34,868
Many digits on both sides also match.

12
00:01:35,318 --> 00:01:40,417
A dot alone does not match. The regex needs a digit next to the dot.

13
00:01:40,867 --> 00:01:46,925
Digits with no dot do not match. Every string in the language has exactly one dot.

14
00:01:47,375 --> 00:01:53,285
Two dots do not match either. A python check with the regex gave all seven answers.

15
00:01:53,735 --> 00:02:03,185
Translation is syntax directed. So first draw the parse tree of the regex. Then apply one template per node, from the bottom up.

16
00:02:03,635 --> 00:02:09,715
The leaves are the letters. Each letter becomes a constant machine: two states and one edge.

17
00:02:10,165 --> 00:02:17,995
There are two repetition nodes, one for each d star. Each one adds two new states and four epsilon edges.

18
00:02:18,445 --> 00:02:27,170
There is one alternation node, for dot d or d dot. It adds a new entry and a new exit, with four epsilon edges.

19
00:02:27,620 --> 00:02:40,036
The concatenation nodes join the exit of one machine to the entry of the next one. The slides allow two forms: merge the two states, or join them with an epsilon edge.

20
00:02:40,486 --> 00:02:44,902
Here is the N F A from the course notes. It has 14 states.

21
00:02:45,352 --> 00:02:55,016
The constant machines are in blue. d, then dot d, then d dot, then d. In dot d and d dot, two constants are already joined.

22
00:02:55,466 --> 00:03:03,615
The first repetition template is in orange. States 1 and 4 go around the first d, with four epsilon edges.

23
00:03:04,065 --> 00:03:11,959
The second repetition template: states 11 and 14 go around the last d, again with four epsilon edges.

24
00:03:12,409 --> 00:03:19,065
Now the alternation template. From state 4, two epsilon edges go into the two branches.

25
00:03:19,515 --> 00:03:26,427
From states 7 and 10, two epsilon edges go to state 11, the exit of the alternation.

26
00:03:26,877 --> 00:03:28,882
State 1 is the start state.

27
00:03:29,332 --> 00:03:32,361
State 14 is the only accepting state.

28
00:03:32,811 --> 00:03:44,865
The notes merge states at each concatenation. So state 4 is the exit of the first d star and also the entry of the alternation. Every part matches a template.

29
00:03:45,315 --> 00:03:54,253
Step 2, the subset construction. The start state A is the epsilon closure of the N F A start state. Begin with state 1.

30
00:03:54,703 --> 00:03:57,839
From 1, the epsilon edges reach 2 and 4.

31
00:03:58,289 --> 00:04:01,511
From 4, the epsilon edges reach 5 and 8.

32
00:04:01,961 --> 00:04:07,102
States 2, 5 and 8 have no epsilon edges. So the closure stops here.

33
00:04:07,552 --> 00:04:10,624
So A is the set 1, 2, 4, 5, 8.

34
00:04:11,074 --> 00:04:18,775
A does not contain 14, so A does not accept. That is correct: the empty string is not a decimal number.

35
00:04:19,225 --> 00:04:23,961
Now take A and the letter d. A is the set 1, 2, 4, 5, 8.

36
00:04:24,411 --> 00:04:29,254
Two states in A have a d edge. 2 goes to 3, and 8 goes to 9.

37
00:04:29,704 --> 00:04:34,056
Then follow the epsilon edges. From 3 we reach 2 and 4.

38
00:04:34,506 --> 00:04:36,639
From 4 we reach 5 and 8.

39
00:04:37,089 --> 00:04:42,039
The set is 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9. It is new, so call it B.

40
00:04:42,489 --> 00:04:46,243
B does not contain 14, so B does not accept.

41
00:04:46,693 --> 00:04:52,133
Take the letter edges first, and then the epsilon closure. Not the other way round.

42
00:04:52,583 --> 00:04:54,354
Now take A and the dot.

43
00:04:54,804 --> 00:04:58,580
Only state 5 has a dot edge. It goes to 6.

44
00:04:59,030 --> 00:05:05,515
State 6 has no epsilon edges. So the set is just 6. It is new, so call it C.

45
00:05:05,965 --> 00:05:09,677
C does not contain 14, so C does not accept.

46
00:05:10,127 --> 00:05:14,010
Next, B: the set 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9.

47
00:05:14,460 --> 00:05:18,727
B on d. Again 2 goes to 3, and 8 goes to 9.

48
00:05:19,177 --> 00:05:24,147
Close as before. 3 reaches 2 and 4, and 4 reaches 5 and 8.

49
00:05:24,597 --> 00:05:31,509
The result is B again. So B on d is a self loop. This loop reads the digits before the dot.

50
00:05:31,959 --> 00:05:37,826
B on dot. Two states have a dot edge. 5 goes to 6, and 9 goes to 10.

51
00:05:38,276 --> 00:05:40,111
Close: 10 reaches 11.

52
00:05:40,561 --> 00:05:42,801
11 reaches 12 and 14.

53
00:05:43,251 --> 00:05:48,328
The set is 6, 10, 11, 12, 14. It is new, so call it D.

54
00:05:48,778 --> 00:05:54,986
D contains 14, so D accepts. For example, the string d dot ends in D.

55
00:05:55,436 --> 00:05:57,655
C is the set with only state 6.

56
00:05:58,105 --> 00:06:05,294
C on dot. State 6 has no dot edge. The result is the empty set. Call it X, the error state.

57
00:06:05,744 --> 00:06:08,709
C on d. State 6 goes to 7.

58
00:06:09,159 --> 00:06:11,186
Close: 7 reaches 11.

59
00:06:11,636 --> 00:06:13,876
11 reaches 12 and 14.

60
00:06:14,326 --> 00:06:19,062
The set is 7, 11, 12, 14. It is new, so call it E.

61
00:06:19,512 --> 00:06:24,803
E contains 14, so E accepts. The string dot d ends in E.

62
00:06:25,253 --> 00:06:29,135
D is the set 6, 10, 11, 12, 14.

63
00:06:29,585 --> 00:06:38,481
D on dot. No state in D has a dot edge. So D goes to X. After the decimal point, a second dot is an error.

64
00:06:38,931 --> 00:06:43,283
D on d. 6 goes to 7, and 12 goes to 13.

65
00:06:43,733 --> 00:06:45,760
Close: 7 reaches 11.

66
00:06:46,210 --> 00:06:48,471
13 reaches 12 and 14.

67
00:06:48,921 --> 00:06:54,383
The set is 7, 11, 12, 13, 14. It is new, so call it F.

68
00:06:54,833 --> 00:06:57,734
F contains 14, so F accepts.

69
00:06:58,184 --> 00:07:01,448
E is the set 7, 11, 12, 14.

70
00:07:01,898 --> 00:07:06,805
E on d. Only state 12 has a d edge. It goes to 13.

71
00:07:07,255 --> 00:07:09,943
Close: 13 reaches 12 and 14.

72
00:07:10,393 --> 00:07:14,787
The set is 12, 13, 14. It is new, so call it G.

73
00:07:15,237 --> 00:07:18,160
G contains 14, so G accepts.

74
00:07:18,610 --> 00:07:23,325
E on dot. No state in E has a dot edge, so E goes to X.

75
00:07:23,775 --> 00:07:27,871
F is the set 7, 11, 12, 13, 14.

76
00:07:28,321 --> 00:07:35,233
F on d. Again only 12 has a d edge, to 13. State 13 has no d edge.

77
00:07:35,683 --> 00:07:40,440
Close: 13 reaches 12 and 14. The result is G again.

78
00:07:40,890 --> 00:07:51,023
G on d. 12 goes to 13 again, and the closure is G again. So G has a self loop. This loop reads the digits after the dot.

79
00:07:51,473 --> 00:07:56,273
On dot, F and G go to X. No state in them has a dot edge.

80
00:07:56,723 --> 00:08:01,075
X is the empty set. On every letter, X goes back to X.

81
00:08:01,525 --> 00:08:07,392
No new sets appear, so the construction stops. The D F A has 8 states, with X.

82
00:08:07,842 --> 00:08:11,810
Here is the full table. It has eight rows, one for each set.

83
00:08:12,260 --> 00:08:19,556
Check 2. Every row has one entry for d and one entry for dot. So the result is a D F A.

84
00:08:20,006 --> 00:08:25,979
D, E, F and G accept, because each one contains N F A state 14.

85
00:08:26,429 --> 00:08:28,989
X is the empty set, the error state.

86
00:08:29,439 --> 00:08:36,501
The notes draw this D F A without X. We keep X, because minimization needs a total D F A.

87
00:08:36,951 --> 00:08:41,431
Here is the D F A, drawn from the table. A is the start state.

88
00:08:41,881 --> 00:08:46,019
B loops on d. This loop reads the digits before the dot.

89
00:08:46,469 --> 00:08:50,736
G loops on d. This loop reads the digits after the dot.

90
00:08:51,186 --> 00:08:55,495
Every red dot arrow goes into X, the error state in the middle.

91
00:08:55,945 --> 00:09:00,361
Run d d dot d. A, B, B, D, F. F accepts.

92
00:09:00,811 --> 00:09:06,785
Run two dots. A, C, then X. X never leaves X, so the string is rejected.

93
00:09:07,235 --> 00:09:17,219
Step 3, minimization. Here is the D F A as a table. It must be total and trim. It is total, because X catches every missing arrow.

94
00:09:17,669 --> 00:09:22,767
It is trim, because the subset construction builds only the sets that A reaches.

95
00:09:23,217 --> 00:09:29,724
Begin with three bubbles. The first bubble holds the accepting states: D, E, F and G.

96
00:09:30,174 --> 00:09:33,886
The second bubble holds the other states: A, B and C.

97
00:09:34,336 --> 00:09:37,216
The third bubble holds X, the error state.

98
00:09:37,666 --> 00:09:44,066
Now write a row for each state. For each letter, write the target state and the bubble of the target.

99
00:09:44,516 --> 00:09:49,103
For example, A on d goes to B, and B is in bubble A B C.

100
00:09:49,553 --> 00:09:56,401
Look inside each bubble. In bubble A B C, the rows differ. The orange cells differ from row A.

101
00:09:56,851 --> 00:10:01,757
On d, A and B go to bubble A B C, but C goes to D E F G.

102
00:10:02,207 --> 00:10:10,719
On dot, A goes to A B C, B goes to D E F G, and C goes to X. These are ambiguous transitions.

103
00:10:11,169 --> 00:10:14,433
So bubble A B C splits into A, B and C.

104
00:10:14,883 --> 00:10:25,059
Inside D E F G, every row is the same: d goes to D E F G, and dot goes to X. So D E F G stays together.

105
00:10:25,509 --> 00:10:28,944
Round 2. Write the rows again with the new bubbles.

106
00:10:29,394 --> 00:10:34,813
D, E, F and G all go to D E F G on d, and to X on dot.

107
00:10:35,263 --> 00:10:39,999
The other bubbles have one state each. A bubble with one state cannot split.

108
00:10:40,449 --> 00:10:43,542
No transition is ambiguous, so we stop.

109
00:10:43,992 --> 00:10:51,587
The course notes do the same work one ambiguous transition at a time. First they split C away, on d.

110
00:10:52,037 --> 00:10:56,709
Then they split A from B, on dot. The order does not change the answer.

111
00:10:57,159 --> 00:11:01,852
The 8 states became 5. Without the error state, that is 4 states.

112
00:11:02,302 --> 00:11:06,057
Here is the minimal D F A. A is the start state.

113
00:11:06,507 --> 00:11:08,917
B reads the digits before the dot.

114
00:11:09,367 --> 00:11:12,034
C means: a dot with no digit yet.

115
00:11:12,484 --> 00:11:16,815
D E F G accepts. It reads the digits after the dot.

116
00:11:17,265 --> 00:11:27,121
Test 1. d dot d goes A, B, D E F G, D E F G. It ends in an accepting state. The regex also matches.

117
00:11:27,571 --> 00:11:33,352
Test 2. dot d goes A, C, D E F G. Accept. The regex matches.

118
00:11:33,802 --> 00:11:39,519
Test 3. Two dots go A, C, X. Reject. The regex does not match.

119
00:11:39,969 --> 00:11:48,225
Test 4. d dot d dot goes A, B, D E F G, D E F G, then X on the second dot. Reject.

120
00:11:48,675 --> 00:11:58,233
A python check ran the N F A, the D F A, the minimal D F A and the regex on every string up to length 8. They all agree.

121
00:11:58,683 --> 00:12:07,579
The second example is from a past midterm. The W language is extended to three values: U for unknown, F and T.

122
00:12:08,029 --> 00:12:10,653
The regex is U or F or T, star.

123
00:12:11,103 --> 00:12:18,185
Here is the N F A from the course notes. In the middle, a three-way alternation reads U, F or T.

124
00:12:18,635 --> 00:12:25,355
Around it is the repetition template. The long dashed epsilon edge from 8 back to 1 repeats.

125
00:12:25,805 --> 00:12:31,309
The long dashed epsilon edge from 1 to 8 skips. Then the string can be empty.

126
00:12:31,759 --> 00:12:37,733
Pause the video and try it. Build the D F A with the subset construction. Then continue.

127
00:12:38,183 --> 00:12:44,305
Here is the subset construction. The start state A is the epsilon closure of state 1.

128
00:12:44,755 --> 00:12:48,809
From 1, the epsilon edges reach 2, 3, 4 and 8.

129
00:12:49,259 --> 00:12:57,536
From 8, the long epsilon edge goes back to 1, but 1 is already in the set. So A is 1, 2, 3, 4, 8.

130
00:12:57,986 --> 00:13:00,205
A contains 8, so A accepts.

131
00:13:00,655 --> 00:13:05,113
A on U. Only state 2 has a U edge. It goes to 5.

132
00:13:05,563 --> 00:13:07,291
Close: 5 reaches 8.

133
00:13:07,741 --> 00:13:09,192
8 reaches 1.

134
00:13:09,642 --> 00:13:16,618
1 reaches 2, 3 and 4. So B is 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8. A on U goes to B.

135
00:13:17,068 --> 00:13:22,188
F and T work the same way, through 6 and through 7. They give C and D.

136
00:13:22,638 --> 00:13:30,425
Each set contains 2, 3 and 4. So from every state, U goes to B, F goes to C, and T goes to D.

137
00:13:30,875 --> 00:13:34,608
Every set contains 8, so all four states accept.

138
00:13:35,058 --> 00:13:40,562
Now minimize. All four states accept. So the first bubble holds all of them.

139
00:13:41,012 --> 00:13:49,673
The D F A is total: every state has an arrow on U, on F and on T. So there is no error state, and no third bubble.

140
00:13:50,123 --> 00:13:57,377
Each cell shows the bubble of the target. Every target is in bubble A B C D. So nothing can split.

141
00:13:57,827 --> 00:14:01,603
So the minimal D F A has one state, A B C D.

142
00:14:02,053 --> 00:14:12,911
That one state accepts and loops on U, F and T. It accepts every string over the three letters, and the empty string too. This is the posted solution.

143
00:14:13,361 --> 00:14:17,073
A python check agrees with the regex up to length 6.

144
00:14:17,523 --> 00:14:22,963
To summarize. Step 1: draw the parse tree, and apply one template per node.

145
00:14:23,413 --> 00:14:35,253
Step 2: the start state is the epsilon closure of the N F A start. For each set and letter, take the letter edges, then the closure. The empty set is the error state.

146
00:14:35,703 --> 00:14:48,717
Step 3: start with bubbles for the accepting states, the other states, and the error state. Write the target bubble for each letter, and split a bubble when its rows differ. Repeat until nothing splits.

147
00:14:49,167 --> 00:14:53,177
At the end, test strings on every machine and on the regex.

148
00:14:53,627 --> 00:15:06,427
The decimal numbers went from 14 to 8 to 5 states. U or F or T, star, went from 8 to 4 to 1. The next video does two more examples, with larger alphabets.
